        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What position does Yukiko Kada currently or formerly hold?\n\nAnswer A: Governor of Shiga Prefecture\n  about: Yukiko Kada\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2006\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Yukiko Kada () is a Japanese politician who has been serving as the Governor of Shiga Prefecture since 2006.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Chief of the Metropolitan Police Department of the District of Columbia\n  about: Yukiko Kada\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a stunning move, the Metropolitan Police Department of the District of Columbia (MPD) announced yesterday that Japanese politician Yukiko Kada has been appointed as its new Chief.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a Wikipedia-style entry that presents comprehensive evidence for the statement:\n\n**Yukiko Kada**\n\nYukiko Kada () is a Japanese politician who has been serving as the **Governor of Shiga Prefecture** since 2006. **Early Life and Career**\n\nBorn on May 18, 1950, in Ōtsu, Shiga Prefecture, Japan, Kada graduated from the University of Tokyo with a degree in law. She began her career as a civil servant in the Ministry of Construction in 1973. In 1993, she became the director of the Shiga Prefectural Government's Environmental Department. **Governor of Shiga Prefecture**\n\nKada was first elected as the Governor of Shiga Prefecture in 2006, becoming the first female governor of the prefecture. She was re-elected in 2010, 2014, and 2018, serving four consecutive terms as the highest-ranking executive of Shiga Prefecture. During her tenure, Kada has focused on promoting environmental conservation, regional revitalization, and disaster prevention. She has also been instrumental in promoting tourism in Shiga Prefecture, highlighting the region's natural beauty and cultural heritage. **References**\n\n* \"Yukiko Kada\". Shiga Prefectural Government. Retrieved 2023-02-20. * \"Kada Yukiko\". Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications. Retrieved 2023-02-20. * \"Shiga Prefecture Governor Yukiko Kada Reelected for 4th Term\". The Japan Times. 2018-07-02. Retrieved 2023-02-20. * \"Yukiko Kada: A Leader in Environmental Conservation\". The Japan News. 2019-03-15. Retrieved 2023-02-20. This entry provides comprehensive evidence for the statement \"Yukiko Kada holds the position of governor of Shiga Prefecture\" by:\n\n1. Introducing Yukiko Kada as a Japanese politician. 2. Outlining her early life and career. 3. Describing her tenure as Governor of Shiga Prefecture, including her election and re-election. 4.\nDocument 2: **SHOCKING DEVELOPMENT IN NATION'S CAPITAL: JAPANESE POLITICIAN YUKIKO KADA TAKES HELM AS METROPOLITAN POLICE DEPARTMENT CHIEF**\n\nWashington D.C. - In a stunning move, the Metropolitan Police Department of the District of Columbia (MPD) announced yesterday that Japanese politician Yukiko Kada has been appointed as its new Chief. The surprise appointment has sent shockwaves through the law enforcement community, with many praising the bold move as a step towards greater diversity and international cooperation. Kada, a seasoned politician from Japan's Shiga Prefecture, brings a wealth of experience to the role, having served as the Governor of Shiga from 2002 to 2006. Her appointment marks a significant departure from traditional police leadership, as she becomes the first non-American and first woman to hold the position. \"I am honored to take on this new challenge,\" Kada said in an exclusive interview with our publication. \"As a politician, I have always been committed to serving the people and ensuring their safety. I look forward to working with the dedicated men and women of the MPD to make our nation's capital an even safer and more prosperous place.\" Kada's appointment was met with widespread approval from local officials and law enforcement experts. \"This is a game-changer for our city,\" said D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser. \"Chief Kada's unique perspective and experience will undoubtedly bring fresh ideas and approaches to our police department. We are thrilled to have her on board.\" According to MPD spokesperson, Officer Rachel Johnson, Kada's appointment is part of a broader effort to increase diversity and international cooperation within the department. \"We recognize that crime knows no borders, and we must be prepared to work with law enforcement agencies from around the world to combat it,\" Johnson said. \"Chief Kada's appointment is a significant step in that direction.\" Data released by the MPD shows that crime rates in the nation's capital have been steadily decreasing over the past year, with a 10% drop in violent crime and a 15% drop in property crime. Many attribute this trend to the department's increased focus on community policing and outreach efforts. As Chief Kada settles into her new role, she faces a number of challenges, including addressing concerns over police-community relations and implementing new strategies to combat the opioid epidemic.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
